{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"gaudy-gangrenous-basilisk","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n`choosy-arthrodic-barbet` removes compaction from the close path because it held the ONE store\nwrite lock for up to 13m53s (measured) and starved every concurrent agent. That lands the P0\nfix but leaves the store with NO standing compaction trigger: ticket event logs grow until\nsomeone runs `rebar compact <id>` by hand. This ticket restores an automatic trigger without\never putting compaction back in the path of an interactive session.\n\n## Approach\nRun compaction in a DEDICATED CLONE of the tickets branch, on a schedule, so it contends with\nnobody: it takes the store write lock of its OWN clone, never the shared checkout's, and its\nresult reaches everyone through the ordinary push/merge path.\n\nThis works because a compaction's output is merge-clean against concurrent event writes: it\nadds one `-SNAPSHOT.json` file and renames folded sources to `*.retired`\n(`_retire_folded_sources`, `src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py:238`), while concurrent sessions\nonly ADD new event files. Git merges an add against a rename+add without conflict. Compaction\nalready anticipates a remote snapshot arriving from elsewhere: `_sync_before_compact`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py:52`) pulls first so local compaction can defer to a\nSNAPSHOT another agent already wrote, and the SNAPSHOT-present short-circuit makes a re-compact\nof an already-folded ticket a no-op.\n\n- A maintenance verb `rebar compact-sweep` walks every ticket dir, folds each ticket whose\n  event count exceeds the configured `compact.threshold` (`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:81`)\n  by calling the existing `compact_cli` (`src/rebar/_commands/compact.py:65`) per ticket, then\n  pushes once through the normal push path (`rebar._store.push`). No new fold engine: the verb\n  is a driver over `compact_txn`, so the snapshot semantics, horizon partitioning and\n  authorship ledger are unchanged and untouched.\n- The sweep is designed to run in a real clone — its own `.git`, cloned from the tickets\n  remote — NOT a worktree of the shared repo, so it shares no index, no `.git/index.lock`, no\n  `rebar-git-op.lock` and no store write lock with any interactive session.\n- Trigger: a SCHEDULED sweep (documented, with the cadence recorded in `docs/concurrency.md`),\n  chosen over per-close detached workers because it is one amortized pass instead of one\n  process per close, has no fan-out under load, and needs no lifecycle management on the close\n  path we just cleaned up.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact_sweep.py` — new: the sweep driver (enumerate, filter by\n  threshold, fold via `compact_cli`, push once, summary output).\n- `src/rebar/_cli/` — register the `compact-sweep` verb.\n- `docs/concurrency.md` — document the out-of-band compactor, the dedicated-clone requirement\n  and the schedule.\n- `tests/unit/test_compact_sweep.py` — new unit tests for the driver.\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_compact_sweep_races.py` — new race/convergence tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Sweep folds every over-threshold ticket in one pass and leaves under-threshold tickets\n  untouched.\n- Double-snapshot race: two sweeps fold the SAME ticket concurrently against separate clones,\n  then both push; assert the merged tracker converges to one valid ticket that reduces to the\n  same state, with no duplicate or lost events.\n- Mixed-version window: a sweep and an OLD-style inline compaction fold the same ticket\n  concurrently; assert the SNAPSHOT-present short-circuit plus `_sync_before_compact`'s\n  defer-to-remote make them converge to a valid ticket.\n- Concurrent tail: events appended to a ticket WHILE the sweep folds it are still present and\n  replayed after the snapshot — the reducer's snapshot+tail ordering is exercised end to end.\n- Signing from a fresh clone: a sweep run in a clone produces the same authorship ledger\n  (`_build_authorship_ledger`) and signed events as an in-place compaction, proving the env\n  identity and signing key resolve outside the primary checkout.\n- Failure path: a sweep whose push is rejected leaves the clone's local commits intact and\n  reports the failure rather than corrupting either store.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `rebar compact-sweep` folds every ticket over `compact.threshold` in one pass and pushes\n      once — a test asserts the SNAPSHOTs written, the sources retired, and exactly one push.\n- [ ] Two concurrent sweeps folding the same ticket from separate clones converge — a test\n      merges both results and asserts the ticket reduces to the same state with no duplicate\n      or lost events.\n- [ ] A sweep concurrent with an inline-style compaction of the same ticket converges — a test\n      asserts a valid single SNAPSHOT and the defer-to-remote path taken.\n- [ ] Events appended during a fold survive it — a test asserts the tail events replay on top\n      of the SNAPSHOT and the reduced state includes them.\n- [ ] A sweep run from a fresh clone signs identically to in-place compaction — a test compares\n      the authorship ledger and event signatures produced by both.\n- [ ] `docs/concurrency.md` documents the dedicated-clone requirement and the schedule, and the\n      full local gate set plus CI's pytest tiers pass.","id":"1fde-becd-942a-4351","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Out-of-band compaction sweep in a dedicated tickets clone"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786594626495678001,"uuid":"319f4821-b890-41b6-a4de-594f367b0d75"}